course: dictionary
lesson: 8984
title: The sitar
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A sitar is a musical instrument from India. It has a long wooden neck, a round body made from a gourd, and many strings that you play by plucking them.
explain: Read the words around sitar. «The sitar has sympathetic strings that nobody plucks; they vibrate by themselves when matching notes are played.» Nobody plucks those strings, so vibrate must mean shake and make a sound without anyone touching them.
explain: Ask what kind of word fits. «Ravi Shankar played the ___» needs a thing you can play, so sitar is a noun that names an instrument. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it makes sense.
fact: A sitar is a noun: one sitar, two sitars.
fact: «Sitar» means a musical instrument from India with a long wooden neck and many strings.
fact: A sitar makes a bright, buzzing, ringing sound and is used in Indian classical music.
fact: «Sympathetic strings» are extra strings under the main ones that ring along when matching notes are played.
fact: Ravi Shankar was a famous sitar player, and George Harrison of the Beatles learned from him.
exercise: «The sitar has sympathetic strings that nobody plucks.» What is a sitar? | *A musical instrument from India | A type of boat | A kind of fruit
exercise: Which part of the sitar is the round body made from? | *A gourd | A plastic bowl | A metal tin | A wooden box
exercise: Find and copy the word that names the instrument: «Ravi Shankar was a famous sitar player.» | *sitar | player | famous | Ravi
test: How do you play a sitar? | By shaking it hard | By hitting a drum | By blowing into a pipe | *By plucking its strings
test: What do sympathetic strings do? | They are made of gold | They are plucked by the player every time | *They vibrate by themselves when matching notes are played | They stop the sitar from ringing
exam: The context says the sympathetic strings «vibrate by themselves»; what does this tell you? | The player must pluck them very hard | Those strings are broken and silent | *Those strings ring on their own, without anyone touching them | Those strings are cut off the sitar
